;;; **W10's second witness (`W10/alt-cap-shared`): a saving between two facts,
;;; behind a full list, which no fix of the cap that keeps it bounded keeps.**
;;;
;;; M1e2 S1e2.2.11. Banked at T1e2.2.11.1 as a probe, promoted into the corpus
;;; at T1e2.2.11.2 on the user's ruling of 2026-11-04 on Q-M1e2.11, (i)(b):
;;; the core promise is the smallest frontier over **every** derivation the
;;; program records, `alt-cap-core.ein` is fixed by keeping each fact's
;;; smallest-closure arrival beside its list (Q-M1e.15's (3)), and this row
;;; stays `open`, deferred to F22.
;;;
;;; ── The shape ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; (x K) is derivable 33 ways through `x-chain`, one premise each, a given:
;;; a frontier of 1 apiece, the per-fact best. It is derivable once more
;;; through `x-join`, over (a K) and (b K): a frontier of 2, the per-fact
;;; worst. (z K) has one derivation, `z-join`, over the same two givens.
;;; (false) needs both, so its smallest frontier over every derivation is
;;; {(a K), (b K)}, 2, through x-join; through any x-chain it is
;;; {(c Cnn), (a K), (b K)}, 3.
;;;
;;; `x-chain` fires first (priority 100), so (x K)'s primary and its 32
;;; alternatives are one-premise chains, and `x-join` arrives at a full list
;;; with two premises and is refused. Any retention that ranks a fact's
;;; derivations by a per-fact metric, premise count, a frontier estimate (Q-M1e.15's
;;; (1)) or the smallest closure ((3), what the engine does since S1e2.2.11),
;;; ranks x-join last of the 34, because its saving is only visible from
;;; (false), between (x K) and (z K). A list of any fixed length drops one of
;;; a larger set of incomparable derivations, and a program can need it.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine says ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   ein solve FILE, 2026-11-04:
;;;     at 3011fae2, MAX_ALT_JUSTIFICATIONS = 32    unsat core (a K) (b K) (c C01)
;;;     since S1e2.2.11, (3)                        the same three
;;;     MAX_ALT_JUSTIFICATIONS = 1 000 000          unsat core (a K) (b K)
;;;
;;; R is empty and exhausted either way, and `(solve :expect (false))` holds:
;;; only the core is wrong. F22 is to re-derive a full list's refused
;;; derivations when the search reaches the fact, which makes the core exact
;;; with bounded memory. The explanation search's own budget has the same
;;; shape past 64 incomparable environments a label, and its cut is reported
;;; (`Explanation::exhausted`); the cap's is not.

(relation c T :why "{?1} has c")
(relation a T :why "{?1} has a")
(relation b T :why "{?1} has b")
(relation x T :why "{?1} is x")
(relation z T :why "{?1} is z")

(c C01) (c C02) (c C03) (c C04) (c C05) (c C06) (c C07) (c C08) (c C09) (c C10) (c C11)
(c C12) (c C13) (c C14) (c C15) (c C16) (c C17) (c C18) (c C19) (c C20) (c C21) (c C22)
(c C23) (c C24) (c C25) (c C26) (c C27) (c C28) (c C29) (c C30) (c C31) (c C32) (c C33)
(a K) (b K)

(rule x-chain ()
  :match  (c ?y)
  :assert (x K)
  :why    "{?y} makes K an x")

(rule x-join ()
  :match  (and (a ?k) (b ?k))
  :assert (x ?k)
  :why    "{?k} has a and b")

(rule z-join ()
  :match  (and (a ?k) (b ?k))
  :assert (z ?k)
  :why    "{?k} has a and b")

(rule clash ()
  :match  (and (x ?k) (z ?k))
  :assert (false)
  :why    "{?k} is both")

(solve :expect (false))
